              Chapter three
            </span>
          

          
            <h2>
              Skin is not a surface. <br>
It is a system that renews itself.
            </h2>
          

          

        </div>


        

        

          
          

          

          


          

            <figure class="
                lvg-truth-chapters__figure

                

                
                  is-fullwidth
                
              ">

              

                <div class="lvg-truth-chapters__image">

                  <picture>

                    

                    <img src="/assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp" srcset="
                        /assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp 600w,
                        /assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp 900w,
                        /assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp 1200w,
                        /assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp 1600w,
                        /assets/media/06-basal-renewal-layer-a31f01dcea.webp 2000w
                      " sizes="100vw" width="1671" height="941" loading="lazy" alt="A person at rest">

                  </picture>

                </div>

              


              


              

            </figure>

          

        


        

        

          <div class="lvg-truth-chapters__copy">
            <p>It is the largest regenerative organ in the body, and it is doing regenerative work continuously: cells forming at the base and turning over towards the surface, the barrier being replenished, the deeper matrix maintained and remodelled. That is not a wellness metaphor. It is physical, it is measurable, and it is happening while you read this.</p><p>Which changes what the ambition should be. Not chasing every surface mark separately, but supporting the conditions the system needs to do its own job — renewal, recovery, resilience. Improve those and the marks are being addressed at the point where they are produced, rather than after they have arrived.</p>
          </div>

        


        

        


        

        

      </article>

    

